Protocol constants and quantization mappings in this foundation are inferred
implementation hypotheses from public behavioral descriptions. They are not
observed device facts, compatibility proof, or authorization to send bytes.
Useful behavioral references reviewed for this offline foundation include:

- Behringer ULTRADRIVE PRO DCX2496/ULTRADRIVE DCX2496LE User Manual,
  Music Tribe, resolved 2026-08-23:
  https://mediadl.musictribe.com/media/sys_master/h84/h96/8849440243742.pdf
  The Serial Interface specification on manual page 31 documents RS-232 as
  115200 baud 8N1 and RS-485 as 38400 baud 8N1. It does not document the
  discovery frame or authorize treating 38400 as a proven RS-232 setting.
- DuinoDCX by Lasse Lukkari, MIT license, revision
  00b9d70d6192e993f31ec94ff0bc20e6e2265b2c (behavioral reference only,
  resolved 2026-08-23):
  https://github.com/lasselukkari/DuinoDCX/tree/00b9d70d6192e993f31ec94ff0bc20e6e2265b2c
  The pinned Ultradrive.h defines a 26-byte search response, and the pinned
  DuinoDCX.ino initializes its serial paths at 38400 baud:
  https://github.com/lasselukkari/DuinoDCX/blob/00b9d70d6192e993f31ec94ff0bc20e6e2265b2c/DuinoDCX/Ultradrive.h#L24
  https://github.com/lasselukkari/DuinoDCX/blob/00b9d70d6192e993f31ec94ff0bc20e6e2265b2c/DuinoDCX/DuinoDCX.ino#L381-L384
  This source does not define the 18-byte response payload semantics or
  establish behavior on Legalab's named unit. Its license is reproduced in
  third_party/duinodcx/LICENSE.
- UltradrivePi protocol notes by geftactics; no repository license was present
  when reviewed on 2026-08-22, so it is an incompatible/uncertain-license
  behavioral reference only and no source code or prose was copied. Revision
  259fa83f6555be6c5707b437dfdcafc90bb19de5 was resolved 2026-08-22:
  https://github.com/geftactics/UltradrivePi

Synthetic fixtures in fixtures/ are original test data. They are clearly
marked and establish deterministic behavior only; they must not be cited as
hardware evidence. The 115200-first/one-38400-fallback sequence is a bounded
Legalab probe policy combining the official line-rate documentation with a
legacy behavioral hypothesis. All hardware claims remain unverified pending a
separately reviewed, query-only named-device receipt.

The transport boundary's 500 ms per-attempt timeout and 26-byte input ceiling
are fail-closed Legalab engineering limits, not vendor timing claims. The
OS-neutral boundary is injected and does not enumerate or open ports. The
Darwin carrier below supplies the explicit Search-only serial implementation;
Legalab owns live hardware readiness.

The Darwin carrier follows Apple's documented BSD serial-device interface and
the macOS SDK ABI; these are operating-system interface references, not DCX
behavioral evidence:

- Apple Technical Note TN2315, resolved 2026-08-23, documents that the Apple
  FTDI driver publishes a BSD serial port and recommends IOKit-based discovery:
  https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/technotes/tn2315/_index.html
- Apple's tcgetattr(3) manual, resolved 2026-08-23, specifically recommends
  snapshotting termios, applying raw mode, and restoring the saved state:
  https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man3/tcgetattr.3.html
- Apple's open(2) manual, resolved 2026-08-23, documents nonblocking device
  opens. The carrier additionally uses O_NOCTTY and O_NOFOLLOW from the macOS
  SDK:
  https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man2/open.2.html
- Apple XNU ttycom.h at revision
  f6217f891ac0bb64f3d375211650a4c1ff8ca1ea, resolved 2026-08-23, defines
  TIOCEXCL, TIOCMGET, and TIOCMSET. No XNU source was copied:
  https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/f6217f891ac0bb64f3d375211650a4c1ff8ca1ea/bsd/sys/ttycom.h

The 25 ms cleanup reserve, explicit `/dev/cu.usbserial-*` path restriction,
overflow policy, and single-syscall write policy are fail-closed Legalab
engineering limits, not Apple or Behringer compatibility claims. The
implementation uses the independently licensed rustix 1.1.4 and sha2 0.10.9
crates as pinned in Cargo.lock; only the small, documented macOS ioctl/select
ABI shim contains local unsafe Rust. Offline tests use injected fake syscalls
and are not named-device observations. Legalab owns live hardware readiness.
